 * File: closed_sets.h
 * Brief: C++17 view of the non-error closed sets, byte-equal to the Python export
 *
 * Description:
 * GENERATED by scripts/gen/closed_sets_h.py from xbrain/common/enums/sets.yaml.
 * Do not edit by hand. A hand edit is reverted by the next regeneration, and
 * until then the two languages disagree about a closed set -- which compiles,
 * runs, and surfaces only during integration, when a peer branches on a value
 * this side spells differently. tests/common/test_closed_sets.py fails on any
 * difference between this file and a fresh render.
 *
 * Why it exists. chassis_relay, quadruped, perception and rtk_driver need the
 * same closed sets the Python processes use, and CLAUDE.md 5.3 forbids anything
 * under common/ from depending on rclcpp or any ROS type -- chassis_relay is on
 * the emergency-stop path under CRL-4 and CRL-5, and could not use this library
 * otherwise. Nothing below leaves the C++17 standard library.
 *
 * Where the values come from. sets.yaml is the single source of truth and is
 * bound to the design volumes by a symmetric-difference metatest, one case per
 * set. That metatest is what establishes the values are RIGHT; the drift check
 * on this file only establishes that nobody edited the copy. The two are
 * separate claims on purpose: comparing a generated artifact with its own
 * generator is true by construction and would prove nothing.
 *
 * What this header does NOT do:
 *   * it does not throw. The Python side raises ClosedSetViolation. Here the
 *     nearest consumer runs under CRL-4, which forbids dynamic allocation, and
 *     throwing allocates -- so Contains() answers a question and IndexOf()
 *     returns kNotAMember. A caller MUST branch on that answer. Substituting a
 *     nearby value instead is the degrade-to-something-close path 11 S13.6
 *     forbids, and it is forbidden here for the same reason as in Python: it
 *     turns a contract violation into ordinary-looking operation.
 *   * it does not say which sets carry an order. Two of them are decision
 *     orders in the contract and the arrays preserve their sequence, but
 *     "ordered" is a property recorded in sets.yaml and deliberately not
 *     mirrored here. A caller reading a rank off an array position would be
 *     inventing a priority for the sets that have none.
 *   * it holds no E_* code. Those are a separate table carrying four elements
 *     per row, with their own deployed header (CFG-CM-3).
 *   * it validates no combination. A domain name and a plane name are both
 *     members of their own sets and nothing here has an opinion on whether that
 *     pair names a legal key.
 */

#ifndef HACHIST_XBRAIN_V6_COMMON_ENUMS_CLOSED_SETS_H_
#define HACHIST_XBRAIN_V6_COMMON_ENUMS_CLOSED_SETS_H_

/* cstddef for size_t, string_view for the values, and nothing else. Every
 * include here is one every consumer pays for, and one of those consumers is on
 * a hop CRL-5 budgets at 200 microseconds. */
#include <cstddef>
#include <string_view>

namespace hachist {
namespace xbrain {
namespace enums {

/* Returned by IndexOf when the value is not in the set. It is NOT a member and
 * must never be used as one. Deliberately not zero: zero is a real position, and
 * for the ordered sets it is the STRONGEST one -- gate_limiter holds estop
 * there. A miss reported as zero would read as the highest-priority limiter. */
inline constexpr std::size_t kNotAMember = static_cast<std::size_t>(-1);

@@SETS@@
/* Membership. constexpr, so a fixed value can be checked at compile time, and
 * allocation-free, so it is usable from the relay hop.
 *
 * Taken by reference to the array rather than as a pointer plus a length:
 * passing the length separately is how a caller ends up scanning one element
 * past the end of a set that was shortened by a regeneration. */
template <std::size_t N>
constexpr bool Contains(const std::string_view (&values)[N],
                        std::string_view value) {
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
    if (values[i] == value) {
      return true;
    }
  }
  return false;
}

/* Position in contract order. Meaningful ONLY for the sets sets.yaml marks
 * ordered; for the rest the sequence is a table's typography and a rank read
 * from it is invented. Indices compare only within one set -- a gate_limiter
 * index measured against a stop_reason index is meaningless, and nothing here
 * can catch it, because both are plain size_t. */
template <std::size_t N>
constexpr std::size_t IndexOf(const std::string_view (&values)[N],
                              std::string_view value) {
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
    if (values[i] == value) {
      return i;
    }
  }
  return kNotAMember;
}

}  // namespace enums
}  // namespace xbrain
}  // namespace hachist

#endif  // HACHIST_XBRAIN_V6_COMMON_ENUMS_CLOSED_SETS_H_
